Consistent hashing limits movement by tying each key to its nearest server clockwise on a ring, rather than to `hash(key) % N`. Adding a server only affects the keys that now fall just before its new position.

**How the sample code does it**

- **Placement:** each server is hashed into several positions on the ring. Each virtual node is a different hash of the server ID to distribute load.[1] Maintain a sorted list for efficient lookup[1] is how the implementation keeps positions ordered.
- **Lookup:** a key is hashed onto the same ring, and the owner is the first server found moving clockwise. If it exceeds the last node, wrap around to the first node.[2]
- **Adding a server:** the new server's virtual nodes are inserted into the ring. Only the keys that now land on those positions move to the new server. Every other key keeps its owner, because its clockwise neighbour has not changed.

**Why this reduces data movement**

With modulo hashing, changing N changes the result for most keys. With the ring, a new server takes over only the arc between itself and its counter-clockwise predecessor. In general, adding one server to N servers moves roughly 1/(N+1) of the keys (general knowledge; the sources don't quantify this). The source material supports the direction of this claim: A sharding function based on consistent hashing can reduce the amount of transferred data.[3]

**Why virtual nodes matter**

With one position per server, arcs can be uneven and one server may take a disproportionate share. Multiple virtual nodes per server smooth this out.

